libggml-cpu, repack.cpp: software prefetch in the compact Q5_K x16 kernel that serves the routed experts' down projection.
DEPLOYED 2026-09-20 (revision f). BIT-IDENTICAL output (prefetch only). GGML_F18_FEATURES bit 3, default off;
GGML_F18_Q5_PREFETCH_BLOCKS sets the distance in block groups (default 1).

The experts were "finished": 42-43 ms per verify graph at the DRAM wall. The per-matrix numbers of the production profile say
otherwise for one of the three matrices (three verify rows, worker 0, 42 MoE layers):
    gate  Q4_K   12.2 ms   ~97 GB/s per socket     the wall
    up    Q4_K   12.2 ms   ~97 GB/s
    down  Q5_K   18.9 ms   ~77 GB/s                1.22x the bytes, 1.55x the time
The compact Q5 kernel (ggml_gemv_q5_K_x16_bytes_impl<NR, true, 4>) walks a 2,880-byte block group by SUB-BLOCK: for each of the
eight sub-blocks it visits all eight 320-byte chunks and re-reads each chunk's high-bit line, so the first touches of a group are
strided and the hardware streamer does not follow them. Expert weights never stay in cache (~19 GB are read per decode cycle),
so every first touch is a DRAM read with nothing in flight. The Q4_K kernel reads each chunk once, in order, and saturates the
bus. One _mm_prefetch per cache line of the NEXT block group at the top of each group iteration hides the latency behind the
multiply of the current group.

Evidence:
  8-layer proxy, op profile over 10-14 verify graphs per mode, same process: down 2.660 -> 2.175 ms (-18%), gate unchanged
  (1.69 -> 1.65). Distance 2 groups: 2.66 -> 2.54. Distance 4 groups: 2.64 -> 4.35 ms, i.e. 65% SLOWER: do not guess this number.
  Text and log-probabilities identical on 32 / 3,861 / 13,231-token prompts.
  FULL MODEL, window w10, Codex fixture through the Paseo wrapper, greedy, modes switched inside one loaded process:
      revision e 19.30/19.68/19.65/19.64   with prefetch 20.16/20.19/20.20/20.15   (+3.1%)
      one text hash, 175 of 214 drafts accepted in every run; log-probabilities identical on a short and a 7.7K-token prompt
      verify graph 120.1/117.1 -> 113.8/114.6 ms; 13,231 tokens of context 16.05/15.98 -> 16.36/16.12 tok/s
  Sampled requests (n=6 each) came out equal, 18.60 and 18.60, with draft acceptance 77.1% against 73.9% by the luck of the draws;
  per cycle the prefetch mode is faster there too. Sampled runs are not used for A/Bs of this size.
